In article <humphrey.715414076@eola.cs.ucf.edu> humphrey@cs.ucf.edu (Darren Humphrey ) writes:
>Has anyone ported nethack to MS windows 3.x?  If not, anyone have any idea how hard it would be to do it using Borland C++?  It seems like just a plain vanilla
>"hack in a window" without mouse commands would not be TOO hard.

Well, that depends on you definition of hard.  The event processing in hack
is all twisted around from what Windows 3.x expects, so you have to do 
a little bit of work  to fit the two together.  Another problem is that 
the nethack code gave version 2.0 of the Borland compiler fits.  The 
pre-procesor and optimizer were serioulsy broke.  Version 3.0 seems to 
be fine.

>
>I COULD just run hack in a DOS window, but it would be nice to have it as a windows app.  With all that memory available, you could probably enable all of the
>features availableon Unix...

Again, not as easy as it looks.  Windows may have a lot of memory, but there
are still limits.  Memory segments are still limited to 64K and the TOTAL
amount of static (initialized and uninitialized) data is limited to 64K 
under the large memory model in Borland C++ and nethack has a lot more
than 64k static data (lots and lots of strings).  You still have to go 
through a lot of contortions to make nethack compile for Windows.  Believe
me, I've done it, and it was not fun.

Anyway, don't worry about porting Nethack 3.0 to Win3.  By the time you 
get it done 3.1 will be out and you will have to start over again :-). 

